## Iceland leading the way

About 30 organizations from different countries take part in the platform, which Iceland leads together with UNESCO. All of them represent languages or cultures that are at a disadvantage in the world of technology.

Among small nations, Iceland has become known for driving coordinated, cross-sector efforts to secure a digital future for its language. Through its Language Technology Programs, Iceland has collected vast amounts of data and developed dedicated Icelandic solutions so Icelandic can be used across major technologies.

![](../../../assets/images/projects/unesco-fundur.png)

The main aim of the partnership is to create a space where organizations facing similar challenges can come together, share knowledge, and agree on common methods for collecting language and cultural data, as well as for presenting and handling it.

UNESCO founded the platform in response to Iceland’s call for greater international cooperation and support among countries facing similar challenges, international organizations, and major technology companies.

The idea was first set out in Íslenska aðferðin (The Icelandic Approach), published by Almannarómur, together with the Ministry of Culture, Innovation and Higher Education (formerly the Ministry of Culture and Business Affairs), and Miðeind. The publication describes Iceland’s journey in language technology and its partnership with OpenAI to teach Icelandic to ChatGPT, the world’s most widely used language model.

## A shared approach to data

One of the platform’s main projects is a knowledge base on how to preserve language and culture in AI. It brings together case studies, projects, and examples of successful methods from around the world, which the platform will publish by theme so more countries can benefit. A country just starting its own data collection effort, for example, could use it to see how others approached the same task.

The platform also builds a network and creates new opportunities for partnership between countries and organizations at similar stages.
